---
/* The designs write some bilingual copy inline as a `data-l="en"` / `data-l="ar"`
   pair rather than through <T>, usually where the two languages need different
   markup around them. Both are in the DOM; this hides the one that is not the
   page's language.

   ponytail: <T> renders one language now, and <html lang> is set by the route,
   so this rule is all that is left — no script, no localStorage, no flash.
   Converting the remaining ~200 inline pairs to <T> would delete this file and
   halve those blocks; worth doing when one of them next needs editing. */
---

<style is:global>
	/* Specificity (0,2,1): beats a two-class rule, so a design's own `display`
	   never resurrects the hidden language. */
	html[lang='ar'] [data-l='en'],
	html[lang='en'] [data-l='ar'] {
		display: none;
	}
</style>
